      "evidence_id": "pdfplumber:abstract",
      "source": "pdf_layout",
      "field": "abstract",
      "raw_text": "A common issue with using secure computation in practice is that its security does not place any restrictions on what an adver- sary can use as input in the protocol. In this work, we focus on the practically-motivated setting of (two-message, labeled) private s et inter- section (PSI), and advocate for a clean and versatile solution to this problem: PSI on authenticated inputs. Our central contributions are summarized as follows. - We formulate a novel definition of PSI on authenticated inputs that has the potential for use in several applications, from content m od- erationinend-to-endencryptedsystemstowatchlistsinanonymous e-cash systems. - We design a concretely-efficient and laconic (i.e., the size of the receiver's message is independent of its setsize)protocolforPSIon authenticated inputs. - We build on our PSI protocol to obtain the first laconic set pre- constrained group signature scheme, improving on that of Bartusek et al. (Eurocrypt 23). We also explore various optimizations to our basic protocol, including reducing the receiver's concrete run t ime,andatradeoffbetweencrssize and message size.",
      "extractor": "pdfplumber_section_boundary"
    },
